course: books
lesson: 48
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VI, part 4: Mr. Toad (the car in the inn-yard, twenty years, and the deepest dungeon)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Toad struts along the high road, miles from home, singing his own praises: brain has beaten brute force! Poor old Ratty — a decent fellow, but not clever. Hungry, he walks into the Red Lion inn in a little town and orders the best lunch they have.
explain: Halfway through, he hears it: «Poop-poop!» A motor-car pulls into the inn-yard and its owners come in, chattering about their drive. Toad trembles and holds on to the table leg. At last he slips out, pays, and goes round to the yard — just to LOOK at it, surely there's no harm in that. Nobody is there. «I wonder if this sort of car starts easily?» Next moment, as if in a dream, he is in the driver's seat roaring out onto the open road — Toad the Terror, Lord of the lone trail!
explain: Then we jump to a courtroom. Toad has been found guilty of stealing a car, driving dangerously, and being very cheeky to the police. The Clerk adds it up: twelve months for the theft, three years for the driving, fifteen years for the cheek — nineteen years. «Make it a round twenty.» «Excellent!» says the Chairman. Twenty years!
explain: Toad is dragged off in chains, shrieking, across the market-place where people throw carrots and jeer, over a drawbridge into a grim old castle, up winding stairs, past soldiers and guard-dogs, to the deepest dungeon of all. The old gaoler's rusty key creaks, the door clangs — and Toad is a helpless prisoner.
fact: At first Toad «had taken by-paths, and crossed many fields, and changed his course several times, in case of pursuit» (pursuit = being chased).
fact: «All Nature joining in a chorus of approval to the song of self-praise that his own heart was singing to him, he almost danced along the road in his satisfaction and conceit.»
fact: Toad: «Smart piece of work that! … Brain against brute force—and brain came out on the top». About the Rat: «A worthy fellow, Ratty, with many good qualities, but very little intelligence and absolutely no education.»
fact: «The sign of “The Red Lion”» reminds him «that he had not breakfasted that day». He orders «the best luncheon that could be provided at so short a notice» and eats in the coffee-room.
fact: At the «only too familiar sound» Toad had «to hold on to the leg of the table to conceal his over-mastering emotion». The car's owners are «hungry, talkative, and gay» (gay = cheerful, in the old meaning).
fact: Toad: «There cannot be any harm … in my only just looking at it!» The car «stood in the middle of the yard, quite unattended», the stable-helps «all at their dinner».
fact: «I wonder if this sort of car starts easily?» He finds «he had hold of the handle and was turning it» (old cars were started by turning a handle at the front).
fact: «As if in a dream … all sense of right and wrong, all fear of obvious consequences, seemed temporarily suspended.» He is «Toad the terror, the traffic-queller, the Lord of the lone trail».
fact: The Chairman of the Bench of Magistrates (the judges of a local court) calls Toad «the incorrigible rogue and hardened ruffian» (incorrigible = can't be made better) and asks how to «make it sufficiently hot» for him.
fact: Three offences: «first, of stealing a valuable motor-car; secondly, of driving to the public danger; and, thirdly, of gross impertinence to the rural police» (impertinence = rude cheek).
fact: The Clerk: «twelve months for the theft, which is mild; and three years for the furious driving, which is lenient; and fifteen years for the cheek … those figures, if added together correctly, tot up to nineteen years». Chairman: «First-rate!» Clerk: «So you had better make it a round twenty years and be on the safe side».
fact: The joke: cheek gets the biggest punishment, much more than stealing. Real courts don't work like this — the writer is mocking judges who care most about their own importance.
fact: The crowd pelt him «with jeers, carrots, and popular catch-words»; school children enjoy «the sight of a gentleman in difficulties».
fact: The castle: «the hollow-sounding drawbridge», «the spiky portcullis» (a heavy gate that drops down), «men-at-arms in casquet and corselet of steel» (helmets and armour), «mastiffs» (big guard-dogs), and «ancient warders … dozing over a pasty».
fact: The sergeant speaks in old-fashioned words: «Oddsbodikins! … Rouse thee, old loon» (= Goodness! Wake up, old fool). Toad ends «a helpless prisoner in the remotest dungeon of the best-guarded keep of the stoutest castle in all the length and breadth of Merry England».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «yeet» = throw hard; «ate and left no crumbs» = did it perfectly; «gutted» (UK) = very disappointed; «rad» (older US) = great; «robot» (South Africa) = traffic light.
exercise: Why does Toad first take by-paths and fields? | In case anyone is chasing him | Because he likes walking in the fields | He is lost | The road is closed
exercise: How does Toad feel on the high road? | So pleased with himself he almost dances | Frightened and sorry for what he has done to the Rat | Tired | Hungry and sad
exercise: «Brain against brute force.» Who does Toad think is the «brain»? | Himself | The Badger, who planned the whole thing | The Rat, for being kind | The doctor
exercise: What does Toad say the Rat has «absolutely no» of? | Education | Money of his own | Friends on the river | Courage or brains
exercise: What reminds Toad that he hasn't had breakfast? | The sign of the Red Lion inn | The smell of bacon from a cottage window | A baker's cart | The clock
exercise: Where does Toad eat his lunch? | In the inn's coffee-room | In the inn-yard, on a bench by the stables | On the road, walking along | In the car parked in the yard
exercise: What sound makes Toad tremble? | «Poop-poop!» | A policeman's whistle somewhere down the street | Church bells ringing | The Rat's voice
exercise: Why does Toad hold on to the table leg? | To hide his overpowering excitement | Because the table is shaking in a storm | He is falling asleep | To stop it being taken
exercise: What does Toad tell himself before going to the yard? | There can't be any harm in only just looking | He will buy the car with the money from his drawer | He will steal it | He'll ask the owners
exercise: Why is the car unattended? | The stable-helps are all at their dinner | The owners are watching it from a window | It is broken | A policeman is guarding it
exercise: «I wonder if this sort of car starts easily?» What happens next? | He finds himself turning the starting-handle | He goes back inside to finish his lunch, very calmly | He asks the owner | He walks away
exercise: How does Toad feel as he drives off? | As if in a dream, all sense of right and wrong gone | Guilty and sorry, driving slowly and carefully along the road | Scared | Sick
exercise: Which name does Toad NOT give himself as he drives? | The Wise Old Toad | Toad the terror | The traffic-queller | The Lord of the lone trail
exercise: Who runs the court where Toad is tried? | The Chairman of the Bench of Magistrates | The King of England, sitting on a throne | The Badger | A jury of rabbits
exercise: Which is NOT one of Toad's three offences? | Escaping from his bedroom | Stealing a valuable motor-car | Driving to the public danger | Cheeking the police
exercise: How long does the Clerk suggest for the theft? | Twelve months | Fifteen years, the worst of all | Three years in prison | Twenty years, all told
exercise: How long does the Clerk suggest for the furious driving? | Three years | Twelve months, which is very mild | Six months and a fine | Ten years at the least
exercise: Which offence gets fifteen years? | Cheeking the police | Stealing the car, which is worst | Furious driving | All three together
exercise: What is the final sentence? | Twenty years | Nineteen years exactly, as the Clerk added | Twelve months | Fifteen years
exercise: Why does the Clerk round it up to twenty? | «To be on the safe side» | Because Toad asked for a round number | Because twenty is lucky | By mistake
exercise: What does the crowd throw at Toad? | Jeers, carrots and catch-words | Flowers, because they like him | Stones | Nothing
exercise: Who enjoys the sight of «a gentleman in difficulties»? | The school children | The weasels hiding in the crowd | The Badger, in disguise | The Rat, who came after him
exercise: How does Toad get into the castle? | Over a drawbridge and under a portcullis | Through a little side-door hidden in the castle wall | By boat | Down a tunnel
exercise: «Rouse thee, old loon.» Whom is the sergeant talking to? | The ancient gaoler | Toad | The magistrate | A soldier
exercise: Where does Toad end up? | The remotest dungeon of the best-guarded keep | A small cell in the village police-station, near the market | Back in bed | In hospital
test: Josh: «Toad didn't steal the car, he just borrowed it for a bit.» Put it right. | He took it without asking and drove off — that's stealing, and the court said so | Right: he asked the owners politely and they lent it to him for the afternoon | The owners gave it to him | He bought it
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Toad planned to steal the car from the moment he heard it.» | False: he told himself he'd «only just look», then the craze took over | True: he had a plan worked out in his room long before he escaped | True, he hid in the yard | False: he never touched it
test: Nikhil: «12 months plus 3 years plus 15 years, that's 30, the Clerk's wrong!» Put it right. | 12 months is 1 year, so 1 + 3 + 15 = 19 — the Clerk is right | Right: 12 + 3 + 15 makes 30 years, so the Clerk added it up wrong | It is 18 years | It is 20 years exactly
test: «Cheeking the police undoubtedly carries the severest penalty; and so it ought.» What's the writer mocking? | Officials who punish cheek to themselves more than real harm | Police who are far too kind to people who steal cars | Toad's driving | Thieves
test: «Even if you only believe one-tenth part of what you heard, and I never believe more myself.» What does this say about the Clerk? | He admits he doesn't believe most evidence — yet gives twenty years | He is a very careful and fair man who checks every word | He believes everything he hears | He is deaf
test: Looking back: in the last part, the Badger said Toad might come back «between two policemen». How does this part fit? | Toad is caught and taken away by police — just as feared | Toad comes back to Toad Hall in his own new car, smiling | Toad is never caught | The Badger is arrested
test: Looking back: in the second part of this chapter, Toad promised: «the very first motor-car I see…» what? | «…poop-poop! off I go in it!» | «…I'll smash it» | «…I'll run away from it» | «…I'll ignore»
test: So Toad did keep one promise. Which? | His promise to jump into the first car he saw | His promise to give up cars for ever | His promise to the Badger in the smoking-room | None
test: Looking back: in chapter II, after the first car, Toad sat in the road «in a sort of trance». Which words in this part are like that? | «As if in a dream» | «Smart piece of work» | «Brain against brute force» | «Rouse thee»
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, the Rat said Toad's end must be «killed or ruined». What has happened to Toad now? | Neither yet, but he's lost his freedom for twenty years | He has been killed in a crash on the high road | He is ruined and poor | He is safe at home
test: Looking back: what did the Rat say about Toad in chapter II, before they met him? | «So simple, so good-natured … Perhaps he's not very clever» | «He's the cleverest animal on the whole river, and very modest» | «He's very shy» | «He's a bad lot»
test: Toad called the Rat stupid: «very little intelligence». Who really behaves with little sense in this part? | Toad, who steals a car within hours of escaping | The magistrates, who kindly let Toad go free with a warning | The Badger | The Mole
test: Cara, a bit gutted (very disappointed): «so the book's over, Toad's in the dungeon for good?» What does the writer hint last lesson? | Much water was to run under bridges before Toad sat at ease in his Hall — more story to come | Yes, the book ends here with Toad in the dungeon for ever | Toad dies in the dungeon | The Rat joins him
test: What would have happened if the stable-helps had NOT been at their dinner? | Someone would probably have stopped Toad touching the car | Toad would have driven off faster with the stable-helps chasing | Nothing at all different | The Badger would have come
test: ★★★ Careful with the Clerk's sum: suppose Toad went into the dungeon aged 30 and served the whole sentence the Chairman gave. How old would he be when let out? | 50 | 49 | 45 | 60
test: ★★★ «The remotest dungeon of the best-guarded keep of the stoutest castle in all … Merry England.» Why stack up so many «-est» words? | A joke — it makes Toad's prison sound as huge and grim as possible | To show that it is really a small, cosy little jail | To teach spelling | It is a list of castles
test: ★★★ Toad says «brain came out on the top». Count: in this chapter, how many of these did Toad do — escape, steal a car, get caught? | All three | Only one | Two of them, but not the third | None
exam: Identify the offence which, in the Clerk's estimation, merited the severest penalty. | Impertinence to the rural police | The theft of a valuable motor-car, the property of another | Driving to the public danger | Escape from custody
exam: Compute the aggregate term proposed by the Clerk prior to its rounding. | Nineteen years | Thirty years, being the sum of twelve, three and fifteen | Twenty years precisely | Fifteen years only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstances in which Toad gained possession of the motor-car? | He started and drove off a car left unattended in an inn-yard | He purchased it lawfully from its owners in the coffee-room of the inn | It was lent to him | It was delivered to Toad Hall
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Toad's place of confinement. | The remotest dungeon of a castle keep | A cell at the rural police-station, pending an appeal to a higher court | His own bedroom | The Badger's house
